//! The harmonizer's numbers, measured from the POP909 dataset (Ziyu Wang,
//! Ke Chen, Junyan Jiang, Yiyi Zhang, Maoran Xu, Shuqi Dai, Guxian Bin and
//! Gus Xia, ISMIR 2020; MIT licence) by `scripts/harmony/measure.py`, which
//! wrote this file. Do not edit it by hand.
//!
//! A chord is `12 * kind + degree`: its kind in `Kind`'s order and its
//! root's semitones above the tonic of the major key the key signature
//! shows (so A minor's chords are counted from C). Costs are minus natural
//! logarithms of how often something came, in hundredths.
//!
//! On the songs kept aside (every tenth):
//! - Plain chords, one chord a bar: from the melody alone 32.3% of cells get
//!   the song's chord and 33.4% its root (31.9% in the key the model hears,
//!   the song's own in 94% of songs); with the accompaniment 86.7% and 89.8%
//! - Plain chords, changes inside bars: from the melody alone 33.0% of cells
//!   get the song's chord and 34.2% its root (32.5% in the key the model
//!   hears, the song's own in 95% of songs); with the accompaniment 90.5% and
//!   92.4%
//! - Sevenths too, one chord a bar: from the melody alone 29.2% of cells get
//!   the song's chord and 33.9% its root (28.8% in the key the model hears,
//!   the song's own in 94% of songs); with the accompaniment 75.5% and 88.5%
//! - Sevenths too, changes inside bars: from the melody alone 29.5% of cells
//!   get the song's chord and 34.2% its root (29.1% in the key the model
//!   hears, the song's own in 95% of songs); with the accompaniment 87.2% and
//!   92.5%

